{"candidates":[{"content":"The assertion that an append-only log requires commutativity of the fold operation contradicts the claim that writer idempotency is strictly necessary for deterministic replay, relocating the deduplication burden from the writer to the consumer. This contradiction is refuted if the log's determinism depends on the writer appending a canonical sequence rather than the downstream consumer's ability to suppress duplicate events during replay.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"18174034ea0c22d562ec2796e71c4dd8954937243fa0d30cc564170b82ce7d34","quote":"For deterministic replay in an append-only log, the required property is idempotency because the log's determinism is an outcome property, not merely a harm-avoidance property."},{"block":"2f8f85328fae446c3c422bd4286bfcd4df6abfd26babf3005820e939993c4d0b","quote":"An append-only event log requires neither idempotence nor safety from its writers in the conventional sense; it requires commutativity of the fold operation over the event stream."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"fe91ea9f2df4d0678d6bb472a0cbb4efb03d324dfabaa8010614ec783870b8c9"},{"role":"dependence","target":"2f8f85328fae446c3c422bd4286bfcd4df6abfd26babf3005820e939993c4d0b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3fc33b3603be4326107111e73ee5bfa735e6f92a6da6a64286fc895870602e42"}],"typicality":0.75}],"simulation_proposals":[]}